---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConfirmOfficial from "../components/ConfirmOfficial.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Buying property in 2026"
  description="Buying in Andorra in 2026: 4% ITP, 6–10% foreign-investment tax for non-residents and new residents, parish prices."
  path="/buying-property-in-2026"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Buying property</h1>
    <p>
      The deed is not a residency card. For non-residents and for
      residents of under three years, the extra tax is now the story.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <ConfirmOfficial />
    <p>
      Companion to the live purchase page,
      <a href="/buying-property">Buying as a non-resident</a>.
      Official rates on that page win if the two differ.
    </p>

    <p>
      Anyone can look at flats on Idealista. The part that surprises
      people in 2026 is the stack of tax and authorisation sitting on
      the price. A long-standing resident paying 4% transfer tax and a
      newcomer paying 4% plus 6% or 10% foreign-investment tax are not
      in the same market.
    </p>

    <h2>Two taxes</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tax</th>
          <th>What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>ITP</td>
          <td>
            Generally 4% of the deed price on second-hand property: 1%
            to the State, 3% to the parish. Paid by the buyer at the
            notary.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>IIEI</td>
          <td>
            Llei 3/2024, rates raised by Llei 2/2026 from 13 February
            2026. Government summary: 6% on the first qualifying
            acquisition, 10% from the second or where the investment
            exceeds the single-home limits. Applies to non-residents and
            to residents who cannot show three years of effective
            residence in the previous ten (study absences can count).
            Paid on top of ITP.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The Government’s 26 February 2026 note on
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/s-actualitza-el-reglament-de-l-impost-sobre-la-inversio-estrangera-immobiliaria-d-acord-amb-la-nova-llei-de-continuitat-i-consolidacio-de-les-mesures-per-al-creixement-sostenible"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Decret 58/2026</a
      >
      is the public summary of those IIEI rates.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A non-resident buying a first home at €1,000,000: €40,000 ITP +
      €60,000 IIEI = €100,000 before notary, registry and agency.
      Confirm the exact taxable base with the notary. Do not use this as
      a quote.
    </p>
    <p>
      Exemptions exist. February 2026 regulation simplified how
      exemptions are shown to the notary. If you think you are exempt,
      get that in writing before you reserve.
    </p>

    <h2>Authorisation</h2>
    <p>
      Official Government publications describe prior administrative
      authorisation for foreign investment in real estate. In the
      Government’s own statistical yearbook the cases include a
      non-resident individual who is not Andorran, and a resident who
      cannot show three years of effective and permanent residence
      within the ten years before the application, subject to
      international conventions. Treat that as a map, not the current
      legal test. The operative text is the foreign-investment law and
      its regulations, as applied by the Register of Foreign
      Investments. A notary formalises a completed purchase. Use an
      <a href="https://www.agia.ad/collegiats" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >AGIA</a
      >-registered agent. We list member firms that publish their own
      sites in the
      <a href="/directory">directory</a>.
      This site does not invent houses.
    </p>

    <h2>What the market actually did</h2>
    <p>
      Official 2025 figures: flats averaged about €4,547 per square
      metre at year end, with large parish gaps. Escaldes-Engordany led
      both price and a sharp rise in transactions. Asking prices on
      portals in August 2026 sat higher than completed averages. Treat
      portal medians as asking, not sold. Non-resident purchases jumped
      after the earlier moratorium; the State then doubled the
      foreign-investment tax.
    </p>

    <h2>Parish snapshot</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Parish</th>
          <th>What it is, for a buyer</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Andorra la Vella</td>
          <td>
            The capital. Ministries, banks, the daily errands. Smaller
            streets, not a ski village.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Escaldes-Engordany</td>
          <td>
            Joined to the capital. Hospital, thermal water, a broader
            residential grain. Led 2025 price and transaction rise.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>La Massana</td>
          <td>
            Western valleys. Pal and Arinsal for ski; the village itself
            is more year-round.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Ordino</td>
          <td>
            Northern, quieter, more woodland than shop front. A short
            drive for larger errands.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Encamp</td>
          <td>
            Working parish on the road to Grandvalira. Pas de la Casa is
            a different, frontier proposition.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Canillo</td>
          <td>
            High eastern valley. Soldeu and El Tarter in winter; further
            from the ministries.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sant Julià de Lòria</td>
          <td>
            Southern gate on the Spanish road. Lower, less alpine, closer
            to Catalonia.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Portal samples move daily. We do not publish live asking prices
      here. Parish character for people who have to stay is on
      <a href="/where-to-live">Where to live if you must stay</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>If the house is also the passive investment</h2>
    <p>
      Read
      <a href="/passive-residency">Passive residency in 2026</a>.
      A unit under €800,000 does not carry the file. A house you never
      sleep in does not carry the 90 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      Related:
      <a href="/passive-residency">Passive residency in 2026</a>,
      <a href="/where-to-live">Where to live if you must stay</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
